      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "Let $a=-63$ and $n=5.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n,$ and determine $q\\cdot r.$",
      "worked_solution": "Dividing $-63$ by $5$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n-13<-63÷5<-12.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=-13.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by solving $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=-63-(-13)(5)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $-63=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-63={{(-13)}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 5+{{2}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $q\\cdot r=-13\\cdot 2=-26.$",
